WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.886
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.950
In a place like MIT, you have
people from all over the world,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.370
from very different backgrounds,
ethnicities, and races,

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:16.870
and cultures.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.530
And the OME is a place where you
can find people that probably

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.210
have your same background.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.137
In my case, there's one
of the staff members

00:00:24.137 --> 00:00:24.970
who is Puerto Rican.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.130
So that's always nice for me
to go in and just talk to her.

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.259
They're open arms, no matter who
you are or where you're from.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:36.850
Coming to a place like
MIT was very intimidating.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:41.020
No one in my family had ever
even been to college before.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.137
We are minorities here.

00:00:42.137 --> 00:00:43.720
There aren't large
communities for you

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:45.630
to fit in and feel at home.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:47.950
And I think that it
can take some time

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.480
to find those communities.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.360
And I think the OME just
expedites that process.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.680
I would say the OME has
definitely made my time here

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.390
less lonely and less confusing.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.290
At the core of this
mission to make the world

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.860
a better place is empowering
students with that mindset,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.260
with that mind at hand
mindset, and giving them

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.300
the tools that
they need in order

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.800
to be effective in that mission.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.490
And the OME plays
a distinct role

00:01:15.490 --> 00:01:20.560
in that in making sure that
these students are successful.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.480
No matter how small
you think the issue

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.120
or how big the issue
might seem to you, like,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.646
the OME is there to
help you in some way,

00:01:27.646 --> 00:01:29.020
even if you don't
think they can.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.540
Like, even if it's
not academic, the OME

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:35.080
is there to support you, all of
you, and everything that like,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.500
could be impacting
you while here at MIT.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.530
For many students, they
find within the OME

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.635
that this is the community that
becomes sort of their home.

00:01:44.635 --> 00:01:45.850
Come on back.

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.017
It's very, very easy.

00:01:47.017 --> 00:01:48.100
Just walk into the office.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.160
Hi, how are you doing?

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:51.400
Cheryl is probably right
there, says hi to you,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.170
and helps you out.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.714
It's just like a
loving environment.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:56.380
You always leave I
think in a good space

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.100
when you're, like,
done with anything

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:00.790
that OME's doing for you
because you can just feel

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:04.210
the love that it comes out of.

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:07.870
The OME host a program called
Interphase, which it invites

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:11.800
incoming freshmen to spend the
summer at MIT, gain community,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.860
get to know the campus, also
have a taste of the coursework,

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.890
to get a jump on
the new semester.

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:20.200
I recall that
experience being really

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.340
instrumental in
helping me to feel

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:25.850
a part of the MIT community.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:28.570
The OME is the perfect partner.

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.910
They develop relationships
with students

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:34.060
from the first moment
they step foot on campus.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.640
So we can use the
expertise that we

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:39.910
have in our support offices
and the relationships

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:42.370
that the OME develops
with students

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:46.940
to make sure we get students
the best support possible.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:50.650
We have a portfolio of about 25
different programs that really

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.350
do help students
navigate MIT, but also

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.560
think about what they
want to do beyond MIT.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.580
Seminar XL is
courses that students

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:05.320
take along side their curriculum
that help them succeed.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.180
It takes a little
bit of momentum

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.520
at times to get
students to overcome

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.310
their natural reluctance to
say, I don't understand this.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.770
I need some help with this.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:17.170
What Seminar XL acts
as is essentially

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:19.030
an extra class for
students to attend

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:20.490
with a few other students.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.020
It's often taught
by an upperclassman.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.570
I struggled in physics
when I was an undergrad.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:29.170
And I really never had any
physics in high school.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.260
So when I came to MIT,
it was like f = ma

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.820
and I was like, mind blown.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.850
And so I took Seminar XL both
semesters my freshman year.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.547
They didn't just get me by.

00:03:39.547 --> 00:03:40.630
I excelled in the courses.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.030
I did well.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:44.950
The Talented Scholars
Resource Room

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.470
is a place for
students to gather.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.570
There is a study lounge there.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:52.360
And we also hire graduate
students and upper division

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.160
students to offer free
tutoring services.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:58.390
Laureates and Leaders help
students to figure out

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.330
what graduate programs they
want to apply to, to visit

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:02.950
those graduate programs.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:04.870
It helps them to actually
go to conferences

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.360
that would be beneficial to
them to make their applications

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.730
to graduate school more robust.

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.100
Every student who is part
of Laureates and Leaders

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.960
selects a faculty
mentor, someone

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.110
who agrees to guide them
through the process of thinking

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.360
about graduate school.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.110
Even practical things
such as affording the GRE,

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.550
like that just was not
possible at the moment.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:28.070
But the Laureates and Leaders
program made it possible.

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.480
The Mentor Advocate
Partnership, the MAP program,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.640
pairs mentors with students.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:36.550
We match them with a faculty
member, or a staff member,

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.030
or graduate student, or a
post-doc who basically is

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.740
there to be a support system.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.010
So we have this holistic
network of support.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:47.410
The Standard is this
community, this cohort,

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:51.110
of young men who want to be a
support system for one another.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.590
They participate in
retreats and workshops.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:57.040
It really helps MIT to be a
place where they feel welcome

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.620
and they feel supported.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.730
Momentum is this hands-on
interdisciplinary project-based

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:06.250
course for first year
and second year students.

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:08.560
I was in Momentum
in IAP of 2014,

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:12.030
and we worked on developing
autonomous quad copters that

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.830
would detect gases
and land on places.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.860
It was an opportunity for me
to practice my engineering

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:19.120
in the team setting.

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.070
And it was also great
because the Momentum

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:22.960
final presentations
take place in front

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.310
of company representatives.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.260
So it was a networking
event right after.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.130
And it's a great way to use
your, no pun intended, momentum

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.220
from your presentation in
your networking activities

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.630
right after.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:38.110
We have 16 representatives
from various companies

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.000
and organizations
who are here today

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:42.580
because they are excited
to meet all of you.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.340
The OME has a number
of corporate sponsors

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.500
and supporters that come
in and talk to students

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.470
about resume writing,
dressing for success,

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.870
and sort of being able to
navigate the job and internship

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:54.490
opportunities.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:56.940
I'm Grace Tang Wang at NASA JPL.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.560
I'm here with the Naval
Nuclear Propulsion Program.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:02.144
I am on the University Programs
Team here at Google Cambridge.

00:06:02.144 --> 00:06:04.143
Really happy to be here
and happy to talk to you

00:06:04.143 --> 00:06:06.160
about any opportunities
that might be coming up

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:06.845
in the future.

00:06:06.845 --> 00:06:08.470
We're lucky enough
at Microsoft to have

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.290
a bunch of full-time
engineers who

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:14.650
are MIT alums, who
are alums of the OME,

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.859
to be able to come back and
work with those students.

00:06:16.859 --> 00:06:18.150
I think that's really valuable.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.830
I think it's special that those
companies come specifically

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:25.570
for the OME and to see
under-represented minorities

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:30.020
Having staff who understands
where they're coming from,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:31.840
where they have
invested the time

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.450
to build a relationship
with students,

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:39.460
they know their background,
they have interacted with them

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:42.041
academically, personally.

00:06:42.041 --> 00:06:43.540
They've spent time
with them outside

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.550
of those formal
programs, as well.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.600
I think there's definitely
a trust that develops there.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.070
And that goes a long
way with the students

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:54.640
coming out and being vulnerable,
and saying, hey, I need help.

00:06:54.640 --> 00:07:00.460
The staff in the OME are very
much committed to the mission.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.860
They're there because they care.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.500
It's an oasis in the
middle of the chaos.

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:08.045
And it's important to have
offices like that, people

00:07:08.045 --> 00:07:10.810
like that, that you feel
comfortable with, that just

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.570
want to help you do your best.

00:07:13.570 --> 00:07:17.290
Because I had this access to
these services from the OME,

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:21.760
I always felt comfortable
going into my exams.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:24.310
I always felt comfortable
completing my courses.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.720
Success and success as
the OME has given it to me

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.410
is all about
confidence and feeling

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.450
like you understand
your environment.

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.940
It's wonderful for me to be
back here as a faculty member

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:36.010
and think about 28
years ago, first

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:38.350
being introduced
to the OME and how

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:40.180
profound an impact
those programs

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:43.150
had on myself as an
individual as well

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.010
as on my professional career.

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:47.350
And it's quite extraordinary
and inspirational

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:49.780
to see it continuing
to have such an impact

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.430
on the lives of young people.

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:56.110
The OME and its
programs say to students

00:07:56.110 --> 00:08:02.180
and to the world your
presence at MIT is important.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:03.070
You matter.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.810
And we believe in you.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:08.260
The fact that the OME
was here when I started

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.650
as an undergraduate in 1993.

00:08:11.650 --> 00:08:15.460
And here in 2018, the
OME's not just still here,

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.860
but has grown and has
expanded their programs.

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.820
Like that, to me, says a
lot about the institute's

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:23.890
commitment to
those of us who are

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.550
under-represented minorities.

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.610
About a generation or two ago
someone stood up and said,

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:30.460
hey, we're going to
make this difference.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:36.130
And they started to carve out of
MIT a place and an environment

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.470
where I can eventually
come in 2010 and say,

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:41.850
I feel a little bit more at
home than I thought I would.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:45.580
To all the people that have
taken the opportunity and time

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:49.330
and the sacrifice in order
to prepare a way for me

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:52.690
to pass through, for all the
professions that partnered,

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.690
and corporate partners
who all pitched in order

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:58.690
to make my experience
a little bit better.

00:08:58.690 --> 00:09:00.190
The reason I have
made it this far

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.110
is because I stand on
the shoulders of giants.

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:04.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

